00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.380
What are we going to do in today's video?

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:13.120
We were still hard coding all our prompts in the back end.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:19.120
We were hard coding our prompts in the back end file.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.120
But you know that hard coding prompts is not a good practice.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.120
We discussed this in the rack application.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:33.860
In agentic application, the penalty of hard coding increases.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.860
The reason is that for every node,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.860
whether it is planner node,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.860
executor node,

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.860
or responder node,

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.860
we are working with 3 different system prompts for these three.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.600
And the other thing is that your agentic applications

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.600
are very dependent on your system prompts.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.600
Like how will your agent's persona be?

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.600
What will he do on the basis of instructions?

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.600
What rules do we have to follow?

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.600
What can your agent do?

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.600
What can he not do?

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.600
All these things are defined in your system prompts.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.340
In general, compared to your rag application,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.340
agentic application system prompts are more detailed.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.340
And they are more focused on them.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:34.340
Because they can affect your application a lot.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.340
Rag application system prompts also affect,

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.340
but not to the extent that agentic applications do.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.340
In the case of rag application,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:45.340
the output format may be slightly different.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.080
It may be that the response generated by LLM

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.080
is not up to the mark.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.080
You may see some hallucinations there.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.080
So the output format may be different in rag application.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.080
Whereas what changes can be made in agent application?

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.080
That your agent does something that he should not have done.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.080
So because your agentic application

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.080
is so dependent on your system prompts,

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.080
so if there are any changes in your system prompt

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.820
and you have not tracked it properly,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.820
then you may see some unintentional behavior

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.820
in your agent.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.820
In some cases, it can even break down your application.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:29.820
So proper system prompt management

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.820
or prompt management,

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:33.820
prompt versioning is very important.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:37.820
So that's why it is said for agentic applications

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:41.820
that managing your prompt through prompt management

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.560
and also versioning your prompt through prompt versioning

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.560
is very important so that you can track here

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.560
that what changes in the prompt

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.560
and what behavior of your agentic application changed.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.560
I hope I could explain this point to you.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.560
So let's move ahead and discuss

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:05.560
how you do prompt management in an agentic application.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:09.560
So let's discuss how we will do prompt management

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.560
in our agentic project.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.300
And for this project,

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.300
we are going to use Langfuse for prompt management.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.300
And you already know how Langfuse works.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.300
We have already discussed this in our rag project.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.300
If you still don't remember,

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.300
then I will give you a quick refresher

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.300
how this whole thing works.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.300
So Langfuse basically does

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:36.300
versioning your prompts.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.300
What does versioning mean?

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.040
So if you have a system prompt

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.040
and you upload it on Langfuse

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.040
then that becomes your V1 of the prompt.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.040
Then tomorrow you made some changes in your prompt

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.040
and you pushed this updated prompt again to Langfuse

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.040
then automatically it becomes V2 of the prompt.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.040
And in Langfuse,

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:04.040
you can easily track what changes you made in the prompt.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:08.040
So you can easily track the changes

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:10.040
that is possible.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:14.780
And not only you can maintain the versions of your prompts

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:18.780
but you can also attach some special labels with your versions.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:20.780
Now what is the benefit of this?

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:24.780
Langfuse says that you do not have to import your prompts

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.780
from Langfuse through versions in your code.

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.780
What you have to do is

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.780
you simply assign a specific label

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.780
like a label is your production.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.520
So whatever prompt version you want to use in your application

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.520
in the production environment

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.520
simply take this production label

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:49.520
and attach it to your particular version.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.520
Let's say we have a system prompt

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.520
which has 5 versions

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.520
V1, V2, V3, V4 and V5

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.520
and currently our application is running on V3

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.520
because we have added a production label on it.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.260
And what we are doing in the code?

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.260
We are simply using Langfuse

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:12.260
and simply taking that prompt from Langfuse

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:14.260
which has a production label on it.

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.260
So from the perspective of the code

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:20.260
we are simply telling the code

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:23.260
to fetch the production label on whichever version

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:27.260
from Langfuse irrespective of which version it is.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.260
And tomorrow we feel the need

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:31.260
to create a new application

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.000
and we feel that we should use version 5 in our application.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.000
So what I will do is

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.000
I will go to the Langfuse dashboard

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.000
and I will attach the production label on this V5.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.000
So as soon as I attach the production label on V5

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.000
this label of production will be removed from my V3.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Because duplicate labels are not allowed here.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.000
As soon as you use a pre-existing label

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.000
on another version

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.740
it will be removed from there and attached on the new version.

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.740
Now what will happen?

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.740
Whenever your application will work

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.740
it will work with this new version of system prompt.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.740
You haven't changed anything on the basis of the code.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.740
Still the production version in the code

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:20.740
you are importing it.

00:09:20.740 --> 00:09:24.740
But because you have switched the labels

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:26.740
now you can work easily with the new version.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:28.740
So what is the benefit of this?

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.480
The biggest benefit of this is

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.480
whenever you change the versions of your system prompt

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.480
you don't have to change the code.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.480
Because what happens next

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.480
we have already discussed this

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.480
that because of the code changes

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.480
you have to deploy a new version of the application.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.480
So you have to trigger a new deployment

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.480
because you have changed the system prompt.

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.480
But because you are working with labels in Langfuse

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.480
so you don't have to redeploy it.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.220
Simply go to the dashboard

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.220
and switch your labels there.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:03.220
And the second benefit that Langfuse gives you

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:05.220
is around caching.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.220
So whenever your application needs a system prompt

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:15.220
it generally doesn't fetch that prompt from the Langfuse server.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.220
Your Langfuse is so smart

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.220
that for the same version

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:21.220
it keeps it locally cached.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.220
What happens with this?

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:25.960
So you make less API calls on the servers of Langfuse

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:28.960
and you keep the cached version of the application

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:30.960
prompt from the local system.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:34.960
And there is a time for this.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:36.960
As soon as the time expires

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.960
there is a fresh API call

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.960
and the latest version

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.960
let's say the one with the production label

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.960
then you fetch it.

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.960
So we have covered this caching concept

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:48.960
in detail in the rack.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.960
So I will not cover it again

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:52.960
but I hope you remember all these concepts.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.700
So from the discussion two things are clear

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:57.700
that there are two major benefits of using Langfuse.

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.700
The first benefit is

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.700
that you don't have to change the code level.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.700
You don't have to change the code level.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:11.700
Simply switch the label in the dashboard

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:13.700
and the current version will fetch it.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.700
Second is that Langfuse automatically

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.700
keeps your prompt in cache

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.440
so that you don't have to make API calls

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:24.440
on the servers of Langfuse again and again.

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.440
And your prompt will be imported from cache.

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:32.440
So now what we do?

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.440
Now we manage our system prompts

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:36.440
in our code

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:38.440
through Langfuse.

